# The M9 Hyper-V check's burst-1 run (tools/hyperv/README.md, "At the M9
# check"; docs/design.md, "Transmit and receive"): m8-release.scenario with
# uart.burst=1 on the command line, so uart writes one byte per THRE seen,
# though Hyper-V's 16550 has working FIFOs. Both M8 runs lost a few bytes
# of one of root's lines during the flood of its summary with burst 15.
# Lines whole with burst 1 but cut with 15 (m9-burst15.scenario) mean
# Hyper-V's FIFO takes fewer bytes after it reports THRE than uart writes;
# cut either way points at the named pipe. The scenario cannot see a cut
# line it does not expect, so the session reads each of root's lines in
# the log: run it twice. Root's line "root: uart.burst=1: uart's burst
# capped at 1" comes right after its cmdline line, but is not expected, so
# that bytes lost there leave the run going. The release kernel prints
# nothing; root issues no exit port, since this is not QEMU, and halts
# after TEST PASS; the script turns the VM off. The typed line is 14
# bytes, whose last 6 only the character timeout delivers.
cmdline: test=release uart.burst=1
kernel: release
exit: success
expect: loader: microL4 loader
expect: entering kernel
next: root: microL4 root task
expect: root: cmdline "test=release uart.burst=1"
expect: root: image frames: slots
expect: root: empty: slots
expect: release: watch (priority 255), uart (priority 200) with the COM1 IOPort and IRQ 4, console (priority 150); exit port none: not QEMU
expect: release: a probe thread's DebugPutChar, DebugHalt, DebugDumpScheduler and reserved numbers 19 and 31 each raised UnknownSyscall with the number in MR18
expect: release: type a line
send: release input\r
expect: release: read "release input"
expect: release: uart:
expect: , 14 bytes received,
expect: , 0 overruns, 0 line errors,
expect: , burst 1, # the cap: not 15
expect: TEST PASS
forbid: microL4 kernel
forbid: bootinfo:
forbid: DEBUG HALT
forbid: KERNEL PANIC
forbid: , 0 THRE bursts
forbid: ignored: not a number
